      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Long before settlers, the Kenaitze people—part of the Dena'ina Athabascan—traversed Ridgeway’s land for over six thousand years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ridgeway_alaska",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ridgeway’s rolling hills and ancient river terraces overlook lakes like Mackeys, Sports, and Loon, drawing anglers and nature lovers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ridgeway_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Subarctic weather in Ridgeway feels milder thanks to maritime air drifting in from Cook Inlet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ridgeway_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Ridgeway sits near the epicenter of the 1964 Great Alaska Earthquake, making seismic monitoring a local priority.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ridgeway_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Ridgeway has resisted annexation attempts for decades, preserving its unincorporated status and local identity.",
